course: religion-ethics
lesson: 59
title: Illness, hospitals and children who are very ill
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most illnesses get better, and many serious ones, even many cancers, can be treated; doctors save a great many lives, but they cannot promise every time, and honest grown-ups say so gently.
explain: Picture Mason at Grandad's hospital bed: tubes, wires and beeping machines make him hide behind his mum, until a nurse explains that the machines help Grandad breathe and let the nurses watch his heart, and Mason holds Grandad's hand.
explain: Cancer cannot be caught like a cold; a classmate with leukaemia is not catching, but his treatment makes it hard for his body to fight germs, and it can make hair fall out for a while, which usually grows back.
explain: Very rarely, a child gets an illness doctors cannot cure; a children's hospice is a calm place with toys and a garden where very ill children and their families are cared for, kept comfortable and helped to enjoy the time they have.
explain: Christians pray for the sick and believe God is with them whether they get better or not; many hospitals have chaplains of different faiths, and people of every faith and none help by cooking meals, sending cards and being good friends.
fact: Cancer is a serious illness, but many people get better with treatment; doctors cannot always say at first.
fact: Some cancer treatments make hair fall out; it usually grows back afterwards.
fact: Hospital machines help sick people breathe, eat or keep track of their heartbeat; the beeps let nurses watch.
fact: Leukaemia is a kind of blood cancer; it cannot be caught from another person.
fact: Children having cancer treatment must avoid germs, because the treatment makes it harder to fight infections.
fact: Teasing someone about how an illness has changed their body is cruel; standing up for them or telling a teacher is right.
fact: Most children grow up and live long lives; it is rare for a child to die of illness.
fact: A hospice cares for people who are very ill, to keep them comfortable and help them live as well as they can; Cicely Saunders, a Christian nurse and doctor, started the modern hospice movement in the 1960s.
fact: Operations have some risks, but most go well, and doctors explain the risks to the family.
fact: «Doesn't have long» means a person is likely to die soon, perhaps in days, weeks or months; doctors often cannot say exactly.
fact: A hospital chaplain is a religious leader who visits patients and families; many hospitals have chaplains of different faiths.
fact: Doctors save many lives and ease pain, but no doctor can stop everyone from dying one day.
exercise: Noah's friend's mum has cancer and has lost her hair. Is she going to die? | Nobody can promise, but many people get better with treatment | Yes, everyone who has cancer dies within a week or two | No, losing her hair means she is already cured and quite well again | Yes, because her hair will never ever grow back again now
exercise: Why has Mrs Evans lost her hair? | Her treatment makes hair fall out for a while | Cancer always eats away at people's hair from the inside | She shaved it all off herself just to look different | She caught it from somebody at school, like nits
exercise: Mason sees Grandad with tubes and beeping machines. What are they for? | Helping him breathe or eat and watching his heart | Keeping visitors and noisy children away from the bed | Recording everything Grandad says to people who visit | Making Grandad go to sleep for ever, so he feels no pain
exercise: Sam in Ruby's class has leukaemia. Can you catch it? | No, you can't catch cancer from someone | Yes, if you sit next to him | Yes, if you borrow his pencil | Only if you share his lunch
exercise: Why must Sam stay away from people with colds? | His treatment makes it hard for his body to fight germs | Colds can turn into leukaemia if they last long enough in a child | His doctors simply don't like people who have colds | He might give them leukaemia through a single sneeze
exercise: A boy calls Sam «egghead» because he has no hair. What should Ruby do? | Say «Not cool, leave him alone» and tell a teacher | Laugh along with the boy so that she isn't teased too | Ignore it completely, because it is only words after all | Call the boy an even worse name back, so he knows how it feels
exercise: Lucy's cousin Jasmine, eight, is very ill. Lucy says kids don't die. What is true? | It is rare, but sometimes children get illnesses doctors can't cure | Children can never die of any illness at all, only old people | Only children who refuse to eat up all their vegetables ever die of illness | Children die only in sad films and books, never in real life
exercise: Why is the children's hospice so calm and nice? | It helps very ill children be comfortable and enjoy their time | It is a hotel for families on holiday | It is where children go to be punished | It is a hospital that has run out of medicine
exercise: Lucy asks if she will catch what Jasmine has. What is the honest answer? | Most serious illnesses in children cannot be caught | Yes, she will get ill with the same thing within a week | Yes, unless she stops visiting and never goes again | Only if she touches or borrows any of Jasmine's toys
exercise: Ben's dad has a heart operation. Will he die on the operating table? | Operations have risks, but most go well | Yes, heart operations hardly ever go well for anyone | No, operations are always completely without any risk | Only if Ben worries about it too much while he waits
exercise: Mum says Isla's gran «doesn't have long». What does that mean? | Gran is likely to die soon, though nobody knows exactly when | Gran is very short, shorter than everyone else in the family | Gran is going away on a short trip to the seaside soon | Gran has only a short time to wait before she gets better
exercise: What is a good thing for Isla to do now? | Spend time with Gran and tell her she loves her | Stay right away from Gran so she isn't sad | Wait until Gran is better before going to visit | Pretend nothing is happening and carry on as normal
exercise: Josh wants to be a doctor so nobody he loves will die. What is true? | Doctors save many lives, but nobody can stop all death | Doctors can stop anyone at all dying if they try hard enough | Doctors never really save anybody; bodies get better on their own | Only doctors' families get to live for ever
exercise: Who started the modern hospice movement? | Cicely Saunders, a Christian nurse and doctor | Florence Nightingale in a war hospital | The King of England in 1900 | A football club in Liverpool
exercise: What is a hospital chaplain? | A religious leader who visits patients and families | The doctor in charge of operations | A nurse who looks after the machines | The person who serves food on the ward
test: A Gen Z teen says, «Cancer = game over, no cap.» («No cap» means «no lie».) What is the truth? | Many people get better with treatment, though nobody can promise | He is right; nobody has ever survived cancer, not even once, anywhere in the world | Cancer is just a bad cold with a long, scary name | Cancer goes away by itself if you just ignore it long enough
test: A boy from London says, «Sam's bare weird with no hair, bruv, he's like an egg.» («Bare» means «really»; «bruv» means «mate».) What is the right response? | That's out of order; leave him alone, and tell a teacher if it goes on | Laugh, because Sam really does look a bit like an egg | Agree with him, so you don't get teased about it yourself | Pull Sam's hat off to see, then go and tell everyone in the class what's under it
test: Mason whispers, «Why is he got all the wires? I don't like it.» What is the best thing to do? | Ask a nurse to explain, and hold Grandad's hand | Pull out the wires so that Grandad looks normal again | Run out of the ward and never come back to visit him | Turn off the beeping machines so it's quiet in the room
test: What would happen if Sam came back to class and everyone with a cold sat next to him? | He might catch an infection, because his body finds germs hard to fight | Everyone in the class would catch leukaemia from him in a day | Nothing at all; colds can't hurt anyone, because they are only sniffles and sneezes | Sam's hair would all grow back at once, from the germs
test: Lucy, shocked, says, «Kids don't die tho. Only old people do.» What is honest and gentle? | Most kids live long lives, but sadly some get illnesses that can't be cured | Right, children cannot die of anything; only old people do | Right, but only children in faraway countries, not here | Children die all the time everywhere, so she just has to get used to it and stop fussing
test: An Irish auntie says, «Ah, the hospice is a grand place, the craic is good there, God love her.» («Craic» means «fun, good times».) What is she saying? | The hospice is a happy, caring place for Jasmine | The hospice is a place to be sad all day | The hospice is a kind of pub | Jasmine is sure to get better there
test: Ben asks, «What if Dad dies in the operation?» What should the grown-ups do? | Tell him the honest chances, which are good, and keep him busy while he waits | Tell him not to be so silly and send him straight up to bed | Promise him that nothing could ever, ever go wrong in any heart operation at all | Tell him the operation is only a quick check-up, nothing more
test: A boy in Atlanta says, «Y'all, my grandma's fixin' to pass, my momma says she doesn't have long.» («Fixin' to» means «about to»; «pass» means «die».) What does he mean? | His grandma is likely to die soon | His grandma is about to pass an exam she has been studying for | His grandma is going away on a short trip to see the family | His grandma is getting better fast and will be home quite soon
test: Josh says, «If I'm a doctor I'll stop everyone dying.» What is the wisest reply? | Helping people is a great dream, even though no one can stop all death | Yes, and one day he'll be able to make everyone in the whole world live for ever | No, doctors don't really help anyone; they just write notes | Only priests and vicars can stop people dying, not doctors
test: A hospital chaplain says, formally, «I am here to sit with you and your family, whatever your faith or none.» Who can talk to her? | Anyone, of any faith or none | Only Christians who go to church | Only patients who are dying | Only doctors and nurses
test: A cartoon shows a sick boy drinking a magic potion and being cured in one second. What is the real picture? | Treatment can take months, and doctors can't promise every cure | Every illness can be cured in one second with the right drink | Potions like that are what real doctors use in hospitals today | Sick children always cure themselves if they rest for a day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the transmissibility of leukaemia? | It cannot be transmitted from one person to another | It is transmitted by the sharing of stationery and other school equipment | It is transmitted through the inhalation of air in a shared room | It is transmitted by mere proximity to the patient in the classroom
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a children's hospice. | The provision of comfort and care to gravely ill children and their families | The confinement of children who have misbehaved at home or at school | The accommodation of families who are upon holiday by the sea during the summer months | The performance of surgical operations exclusively, upon children alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the loss of hair during cancer treatment. | It is an effect of the treatment and the hair ordinarily regrows | It signifies that the patient has been entirely cured of the disease | It is caused by contact with other patients upon the hospital ward | It is permanent in every instance and the hair never returns
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression «she does not have long» as employed in respect of a gravely ill grandmother. | That death is likely to occur within a short period | That she is of short stature | That she is shortly to depart upon a journey | That her recovery is imminent
